WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.252
Tracy Clayton: I had this
moment, and I think I tweeted

00:00:02.252 --> 00:00:05.130
it, but I was like, "I can't
believe it took me so long

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:09.801
to realize that you are, like,
what I want to be, you know?"

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:11.094
Jackee Harry: Really?

00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:12.429
Tracy: Yes, oh my gosh.

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:13.722
Jackee: Going to make me cry.

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:15.015
I'm like, "Really?
Wow."

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:18.936
Tracy: But really I was
just like, "She's hilarious,

00:00:18.936 --> 00:00:22.147
and smart, and outspoken,
and just--"

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:23.690
And also funny.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.317
It makes me so mad
people are like,

00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:26.693
"Women aren't funny,
there are no funny women."

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.528
I'm just like, "How dare you
when Jackee is in the world,

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:29.863
how dare you?"

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.657
Jackee: Well, you know
what they mean, though.

00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:34.785
It's a very male-oriented
thing, standup for sure.

00:00:34.785 --> 00:00:37.537
There used to be
a lot more women,

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:39.206
Totie Fields, Lucille Ball.

00:00:39.206 --> 00:00:41.959
I'm talking about
Phyllis Diller, Joan Rivers.

00:00:41.959 --> 00:00:45.379
It was a lot, Imogene Coca.

00:00:45.379 --> 00:00:47.130
I know my history, you know.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.841
But I don't know, it's
like they disappeared,

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:51.802
and now they're coming back.

00:00:51.802 --> 00:00:55.889
But the thing with women is
it's a female thing, but also,

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:58.892
you know, you have these
things you can't talk about.

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:01.687
You know, you know,
that aren't allowed.

00:01:01.687 --> 00:01:07.276
Men can talk about anything, you
know, but women, you have to be

00:01:07.276 --> 00:01:10.028
careful because people are
looking at the woman first

00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:11.280
and then the comedian.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.073
The man comes on, boom.

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:14.533
So, you got to go
right to funny.

00:01:14.533 --> 00:01:17.327
It's almost like you got to
go right to funny, you know?

00:01:17.327 --> 00:01:20.122
So yeah, it's like
Ellen DeGeneres,

00:01:20.122 --> 00:01:21.581
she goes right to funny.

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:23.000
See what I mean?

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.251
Tracy: I see, I see.

00:01:24.251 --> 00:01:26.461
Jackee: And so
we--but we're getting better.

00:01:26.461 --> 00:01:29.381
But I don't know, it's
just one of those things.

00:01:29.381 --> 00:01:31.842
Tracy: Who were some of
your early comedic inspirations?

00:01:31.842 --> 00:01:34.261
I feel like you could
probably just name them.

00:01:34.261 --> 00:01:36.763
Jackee: Lucille Ball.
I love Lucy for real.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.098
Tracy: Since you say
Lucille Ball, it's like

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:39.641
of course, I can see
the influence now.

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:43.270
Jackee: Uh huh, Mae West
for sure, Joan Rivers.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.772
Joan taught me to write down
everything, every joke,

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:47.274
every ad-lib.

00:01:47.274 --> 00:01:49.359
And Rodney Dangerfield is
my last mentor.

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:50.902
Tracy: Really?

00:01:50.902 --> 00:01:52.779
Jackee: He'd, "Baby, baby,
baby, write down everything.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.072
Baby, baby, you got to get
your mantra,

00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:56.283
got to get your mantra, baby.

00:01:56.283 --> 00:01:58.702
You know, no respect, baby.
I got no respect."

00:01:58.702 --> 00:02:02.497
You know, you got to have
your--mine of course is...

00:02:02.497 --> 00:02:05.167
[giggling]

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:07.711
And when you do people
copying, I know you copying.

00:02:07.711 --> 00:02:10.881
And keep repeating it and
repeating it and repeating it.

00:02:10.881 --> 00:02:13.133
And then you put it
in there, you know?

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.344
You got to hammer it out.

00:02:15.344 --> 00:02:18.680
Tracy: I wanted to know
what baby Jackee was like.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.099
Like, did you just, like, come
out of the womb funny

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:22.642
and just, like, amazing?

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:25.395
Jackee: No, shy,
very shy, very quiet.

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:27.606
Singing along to the radio.

00:02:27.606 --> 00:02:30.442
My earliest memory's maybe
seven or eight, singing along

00:02:30.442 --> 00:02:34.154
to the radio, the actual radio
in the house.

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:35.947
We didn't have--yeah.

00:02:35.947 --> 00:02:40.077
And TV baby, oh yeah.
Still am.

00:02:40.077 --> 00:02:41.328
Tracy: Yeah?

00:02:41.328 --> 00:02:42.662
Jackee: Oh yeah, 24/7.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:43.914
Tracy: How did
you end up acting?

00:02:43.914 --> 00:02:45.499
Like, how did
you--did you grow out of it?

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:46.708
Did you break out of it?
Did you act--

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:50.712
Jackee: I was a member of the
glee club from the age of six,

00:02:50.712 --> 00:02:53.090
five, you know, singing.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.343
And all through school, all
the way up, I got to junior high

00:02:56.343 --> 00:02:58.220
and I got in the play,
school play.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.973
And the first one was
"Oklahoma,"

00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:03.183
I didn't get in
the lead in that.

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:04.434
I was mad.

00:03:04.434 --> 00:03:05.685
I didn't get the lead
'cause--and I was crying

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:06.937
'cause I was so good.

00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:08.355
They said, "No,
you're not a senior."

00:03:08.355 --> 00:03:11.316
So, I did "The King and I," and
I got on stage and I was great.

00:03:11.316 --> 00:03:14.403
And I got the applause,
and that's when I came.

00:03:14.403 --> 00:03:16.446
But I still was shy,
I couldn't speak out.

00:03:16.446 --> 00:03:18.657
But then I went and got
acting lessons with

00:03:18.657 --> 00:03:20.867
Dick Anthony Williams at
Henry Street Settlement.

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:23.703
And they taught me, you know,
what you call monologues and

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:26.331
things, and how to get up
in front of people and talk.

00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:28.500
It took a year and a half
for me to get up.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.168
But when I broke--

00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:31.545
Tracy: You broke.

00:03:31.545 --> 00:03:34.005
Jackee: And that's what you
have to do if you're going

00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:35.298
to do it.

00:03:35.298 --> 00:03:36.675
'Cause even now, you have
some actors who are shy,

00:03:36.675 --> 00:03:38.218
but I can speak in
front of anybody.

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:40.387
You know, but don't
ask me to do standup.

00:03:40.387 --> 00:03:42.681
You know, I'm strong in
anything, I'm a confident woman,

00:03:42.681 --> 00:03:44.391
but standup?

00:03:44.391 --> 00:03:45.600
Tracy: That's
a different beast.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.810
That is a different monster.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:48.437
And I tell people all the time,
like, being funny on Twitter

00:03:48.437 --> 00:03:49.771
is one thing.

00:03:49.771 --> 00:03:51.898
Standing in front of a crowd
and trying to make them--I don't

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:53.358
understand how anybody does it.

00:03:53.358 --> 00:03:55.193
Jackee: But I was a sweet kid
and really nice.

00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:56.778
And I still am, you know.

00:03:56.778 --> 00:03:58.071
Bookworm.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:00.323
You know, nerdy, corny.

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:03.994
But I had a nice--you know,
I'm a baby boomer, so I had

00:04:03.994 --> 00:04:07.372
a nice growing up period,
not tumultuous.

00:04:07.372 --> 00:04:10.500
You know, nothing, I mean,
you know, to speak of.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.043
I was a really sweet kid, yeah.

00:04:12.043 --> 00:04:14.796
Tracy: I feel like the '90s--in
the '90s,

00:04:14.796 --> 00:04:16.756
TV used to be so black.

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:19.217
You know, like there
were so many black sitcoms.

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:21.094
There was "Living Single," there
was "Martin," there was

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:22.929
"Sister, Sister,"
there was--what do

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:24.306
you think happened?

00:04:24.306 --> 00:04:26.141
Like, where did all
the black shows go?

00:04:26.141 --> 00:04:27.559
Jackee: You know, we
were in fashion because like

00:04:27.559 --> 00:04:29.936
blacksploitation in the '70s,
all those black movies,

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.272
which were not great, but
they--we enjoyed them.

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:33.690
Tracy: Were necessary also,
you know?

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:37.444
Jackee: What, "Shaft"
and "The Mack,"

00:04:37.444 --> 00:04:41.072
you can go--"Superfly,"
on and on and on.

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:44.576
But we just--we're in
and then we're out.

00:04:44.576 --> 00:04:47.954
Black people, we're in, we're
hot, but we always resurface

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:52.000
because the creativity
is so original, you know?

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.837
But black and white people,
I'm sorry, we're all the same

00:04:55.837 --> 00:04:58.006
because we
influence each other now.

00:04:58.006 --> 00:04:59.966
'Cause I sure have learned
something from

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:02.135
my little young man.

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:04.513
Tracy: Oh, do tell.

00:05:04.513 --> 00:05:06.681
Jackee: It's give and take
now, and that comes from

00:05:06.681 --> 00:05:08.517
the evolution and the evolving
of people.

00:05:08.517 --> 00:05:11.144
So, I believe in evolving,
and that's what's happening.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:13.063
You have to evolve constantly.

00:05:13.063 --> 00:05:16.066
To understand racism, to
understand gay rights being,

00:05:16.066 --> 00:05:18.026
you know, infringed,
you have to learn it.

00:05:18.026 --> 00:05:19.361
It's not--you don't just go,
"Oh, that's wrong,"

00:05:19.361 --> 00:05:20.779
'cause you said it.

00:05:20.779 --> 00:05:23.198
You understand it,
you can fight it.

00:05:23.198 --> 00:05:25.075
Tracy: Right, and you have
to do a lot of listening too.

00:05:25.075 --> 00:05:26.535
Jackee: It's like
Colin Kaepernick in the NFL.

00:05:26.535 --> 00:05:29.788
I understand that, so
you can fight for it.

00:05:29.788 --> 00:05:32.666
But if you don't understand, you
know, you really are confused.

00:05:32.666 --> 00:05:36.127
So, we have to keep evolving.
You feel me?

00:05:36.127 --> 00:05:37.420
Tracy: I feel you, girl,
I feel you.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:41.841
So, what is the greatest piece
of advice that you've gotten

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:44.094
about acting?

00:05:44.094 --> 00:05:47.931
Jackee: From Debbie Morgan,
you know who that is?

00:05:47.931 --> 00:05:51.643
When you leave an audition,
leave it there.

00:05:51.643 --> 00:05:53.979
Don't take it with you home,
don't take it back.

00:05:53.979 --> 00:06:00.193
It took me 40 years to learn
that because I'd get all,

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.237
"Did I get it?
What did I do?"

00:06:02.237 --> 00:06:04.614
I'd be in the car, I'm talking
about it would last for months.

00:06:04.614 --> 00:06:06.324
You, ooh God.

00:06:06.324 --> 00:06:07.826
And then somebody gets that
role, and you watch it, go,

00:06:07.826 --> 00:06:09.369
"How'd she get that part?"

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:10.662
Tracy: "I'm better than that."

00:06:10.662 --> 00:06:12.163
Jackee: Yes, envy, jealousy.

00:06:12.163 --> 00:06:13.748
You know the difference
between jealousy and envy?

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:15.542
Envy is, "Why not me?
Why her and not me?"

00:06:15.542 --> 00:06:18.044
Jealousy is, "I hate her."

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:20.130
No, really,
that's the difference.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:21.881
Tracy: I'm learning so much.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.009
Jackee: So, that's the greatest
piece of advice,

00:06:24.009 --> 00:06:26.303
and I have followed,
and it works.

00:06:26.303 --> 00:06:28.597
Tracy: But it's a practice
to get that, huh?

00:06:28.597 --> 00:06:29.973
Jackee: Just
like forgiveness does.

00:06:29.973 --> 00:06:31.975
When you forgive somebody,
you have to practice it,

00:06:31.975 --> 00:06:33.268
and it works.

00:06:33.268 --> 00:06:35.687
Mm-hmm, but you
have to practice.

